  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1215 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nCompact vigorous shortwave was centered over central Kansas at\nmidday, exiting the DDC CWA. Associated deformation zone\nrain showers southeast of Dodge City will diminish rapidly\nthrough mid afternoon as the shortwave departs, and subsidence\nclears the sky rapidly. With a few hours of strong insolation,\ntemperatures will warm into the 50s this afternoon. \n\nNear ideal radiational cooling is expected tonight through\nsunrise Friday, with a widespread light freeze and widespread\nfrost forecast. 12z RRFS appears reasonable with temperatures of\n30-32 common at 7 am Friday, especially west of US 283 and in\nthe colder locales such as along the Arkansas river valley. \nSurface ridging near 1025 mb will be centered over the eastern\nzones at 7 am, as such eastern zones will cool well down through\nthe 30s as well. Being in November, no frost or freeze headlines\nwill be issued. That stated, many locations have not received a\nkilling freeze yet this season, and Friday morning's\ntemperatures are expected to end the growing season at least\npartially, with some locations as cold as 28 briefly near\nsunrise. With recent rainfall and wet surfaces/topsoil, frost \nwill be common Friday morning, particularly with the favorable \nnearly calm winds. \n\nThe next weak shortwave over Arizona Friday is modeled to track\nwell south of SW KS through this weekend, through Mexico and \nTexas. All of SW KS will be dry through Monday, with afternoon \ntemperatures showing a slow warming trend, returning to the \nlower 70s by Monday afternoon. \n\nTuesday through next Friday - all of next week - will feature\nbeautiful quiet fall weather. Global models depict an\nimpressively quiet synoptic regime, with zero precipitation, no\nstorm systems, no cold fronts, no strong winds, and no killing\nfreezes. Put another way, no weather impacts are expected into\nmid November.",
